          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
