          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-x{y}^{3}.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-x,\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{-2}=-x.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z=2x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-2,\\,Q(x)=2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot (2x) \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z{e}^{-2x} & =2x{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x}) & =2x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-2x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}(2x+1)+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-2}=\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
