          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-109177",
          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\u2217)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$"
